WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
Block two.

2
00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.840
Convolutional networks on biological sequences.

3
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.120
Everything in Block one was about shapes and counting, with pictures as the example.

4
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.440
Now we apply it to the data this course is actually about: DNA, and protein.

5
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:20.120
And we will do something in this block that you rarely get to do with a neural network.

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:26.360
We will open one up and look at what it learned, and find a piece of biology inside it that nobody put there.

7
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.160
First, a map of what happened, because it happened fast.

8
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.480
AlexNet was twenty twelve.

9
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:41.080
Look at the dates on this timeline: within three years, convolutional networks were in essentially every branch of computational biology.

10
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:53.920
On DNA, DeepBind in twenty fifteen, which learned the binding preferences of DNA-binding and RNA-binding proteins, and could display what it had learned as position weight matrices, which we come to on the next slide.

11
00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:01.760
DeepSEA, also twenty fifteen, which read a thousand bases of sequence and predicted nine hundred and nineteen different chromatin features at once.

12
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:13.000
On images, U-Net in twenty fifteen, which became and remains the standard architecture for segmentation, that is, for saying which pixels belong to which cell or which tissue.

13
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:23.560
And ImageNet-pretrained networks fine-tuned on retinal photographs, skin lesions or pathology slides, reaching expert level on the specific tasks they were trained for.

14
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:36.960
And later work found something lovely, which we will see for ourselves in a few slides: many of the filters these networks learned matched known transcription-factor motifs, rediscovered from sequence alone, without anyone supplying them.

15
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:44.040
One limitation is already visible on this timeline, and it sets up Session eleven.

16
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:51.120
Reaching further along the sequence meant larger and larger receptive fields, and eventually that approach ran out.

17
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.560
What replaced it was the Transformer, which relates distant positions directly.

18
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.840
That is Session eleven's subject.

19
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.680
This slide is my favourite connection in the course, because it joins the newest thing we do to one of the oldest.

20
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.920
Think about what a first-layer filter on one-hot DNA actually is.

21
00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.680
The input has four channels, one per base.

22
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.320
The kernel has some width k.

23
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:23.880
So the filter is a grid of four by k numbers: one weight for each base at each position of its window.

24
00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.520
Now, what do you do with it?

25
00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:33.200
You slide it along the sequence, and at each position you add up the weights corresponding to the bases that are actually there.

26
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.960
Go back to Session five.

27
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:45.440
A position weight matrix is a table with one row per base and one column per position in a motif, saying how much an A at position three counts for or against.

28
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.080
You score a sequence by sliding it along and adding up the entries that match.

29
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.120
Those are the same operation.

30
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.520
Not similar: the same.

31
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:59.400
A first-layer convolutional filter on DNA is a position weight matrix.

32
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.520
There is exactly one difference, and it is where it comes from.

33
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:17.160
The classical position weight matrix is counted: you take a set of known binding sites, line them up, count how often each base appears at each position, and turn those counts into log-odds scores.

34
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:26.600
The convolutional filter is learned: it starts random, and gradient descent adjusts it until it helps the network answer the question it is being trained on.

35
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:34.200
So the generation of methods you learned in the first half of this course and the generation you are learning now are not as far apart as they look.

36
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.080
The representation is identical.

37
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.840
What changed is where the numbers come from, and that you can stack many of them and put more layers on top.

38
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:50.840
DeepSEA is worth one slide on its own, because of one move it makes that is worth carrying with you.

39
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:59.520
What it does first is straightforward enough: it reads a thousand base pairs of sequence, and predicts nine hundred and nineteen chromatin features simultaneously.

40
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:09.400
Chromatin features here means things like whether a particular protein binds in this region, or whether the DNA is accessible, measured in a particular cell type.

41
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:16.400
So out of one sequence comes a profile of nine hundred and nineteen predictions about what happens there.

42
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.320
Now the move.

43
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:26.000
Suppose you have a variant, a single base change, that sits outside any gene, so it changes no protein at all.

44
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:34.320
Most of the variants associated with human disease are like this, and interpreting them is genuinely hard, because you cannot reason about an amino acid that changed.

45
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.480
What DeepSEA does is this.

46
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.520
Take the reference sequence, run it through, get nine hundred and nineteen numbers.

47
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.520
Take the same sequence with the variant in it, run it through, get nine hundred and nineteen numbers.

48
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.920
Compare them.

49
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.200
The difference is the predicted effect of that variant.

50
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.120
Think about what that means.

51
00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.800
You have a trained model, so you can ask it what if.

52
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.360
You can make a change that nobody has ever observed in a patient, and get a prediction of what it would do.

53
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:14.560
That is a different kind of tool from a database lookup, and it is why sequence models matter beyond their accuracy numbers.

54
00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.600
The quiz bank asks you exactly this.

55
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:26.200
The limitation is the one from the previous slide: a thousand bases is not very far along a chromosome, and regulatory elements can act from much further away.

56
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:32.280
Getting further needed larger receptive fields, and eventually Transformers.

57
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.760
Now let us build one ourselves, on the data you already know.

58
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:45.240
This is the same problem as Sessions eight and nine: given a protein's amino-acid sequence, predict which compartment of the cell it ends up in, out of five classes.

59
00:05:45.240 --> 00:06:04.720
The same proteins, and, importantly, the same homology-aware split: four hundred and eighty-six proteins for training, a hundred and five for validation, a hundred and nine for testing, split by MMseqs2 clusters at thirty per cent identity so that close relatives cannot sit on both sides.

60
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:13.960
That matters, and Session eight explained why: if a protein in your test set has a close relative in training, you are measuring memory, not generalisation.

61
00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.960
The architecture is small enough to describe in one breath.

62
00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:25.280
Two convolutional layers, each with seven-residue filters, thirty-two filters in the first and sixty-four in the second, with max pooling between them.

63
00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:33.080
Then global average pooling, which, as we established in Block one, throws position away and leaves one number per filter.

64
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.960
Then a linear layer to the five classes.

65
00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:44.240
Nineteen thousand two hundred and thirty-seven weights, against the flat network's hundred and ninety-two thousand three hundred and eighty-nine.

66
00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.080
A tenth.

67
00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.680
Note what we have deliberately chosen.

68
00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:55.920
Global average pooling means this network is position-invariant by construction.

69
00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.680
We are telling it: tell me whether a feature occurred, not where.

70
00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.040
Remember that choice.

71
00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:06.600
In four slides it comes back to bite us, and that is the point of this block.

72
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:14.160
Before the result, one slide on how it trains, because the contrast with Session nine is instructive.

73
00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:22.560
In Session nine, the flat network on this same data reached a training accuracy of one point zero zero zero within a few epochs.

74
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.000
One point zero.

75
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.320
It got every single training protein right.

76
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:34.120
And its validation accuracy peaked at nought point four eight six, in epoch five, and then got worse.

77
00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.880
That pattern has a name: it is overfitting, in its purest form.

78
00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:51.600
A hundred and ninety-two thousand free weights and four hundred and eighty-six training proteins is more than enough capacity to simply memorise the answers, and once the network has done that it has learned nothing transferable.

79
00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.560
Now this network.

80
00:07:53.560 --> 00:08:02.480
Its best validation accuracy is nought point six one nine, at epoch seventy-two, and at that point its training accuracy is nought point six six seven.

81
00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:10.160
Look at those two numbers together: nought point six six seven on training, nought point six one nine on validation.

82
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.440
They are close.

83
00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.080
The network is no longer memorising.

84
00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:24.520
It cannot, because it does not have enough free parameters to memorise with, and what it does have is forced to be reused at every position.

85
00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:33.760
Early stopping is what the curve on this slide shows: you watch validation accuracy while training, and you keep the weights from the epoch where it was best, rather than the weights you end up with.

86
00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:35.880
Here, epoch seventy-two.

87
00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.920
And here is the result.

88
00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:41.360
Every metric, with a tenth of the weights.

89
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:48.120
Weights: nineteen thousand two hundred and thirty-seven, against a hundred and ninety-two thousand three hundred and eighty-nine.

90
00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.920
Test accuracy: nought point five eight seven against nought point five one four.

91
00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:59.120
F1: nought point five eight six against nought point four nine three.

92
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:05.080
Matthews correlation coefficient: nought point four eight four against nought point three nine four.

93
00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:11.200
Area under the ROC curve: nought point eight six nine against nought point eight three one.

94
00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.920
A word on that third one, since it is the one students ask about.

95
00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:21.240
The Matthews correlation coefficient exists because accuracy alone misleads when the classes are unequal.

96
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:30.800
If ninety per cent of your proteins are cytoplasmic, answering cytoplasmic every time scores ninety per cent accuracy and has learned nothing.

97
00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:38.120
MCC takes all classes into account and runs from minus one to one, where zero is what chance scores.

98
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.680
Nought point four eight is real signal on a genuinely hard problem.

99
00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.680
Why does the smaller network win?

100
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:51.560
Because it builds in an assumption instead of learning a hundred and ninety-two thousand free weights from four hundred and eighty-six proteins.

101
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:59.080
The assumption is the one from the first slide: the same local pattern can be detected with the same weights wherever it occurs.

102
00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:06.800
The flat network had to discover that from data, and with four hundred and eighty-six examples it could not.

103
00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:12.600
Now one honest caveat, which I think is worth more to you than the result itself.

104
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:21.240
There is a third model on that table: plain logistic regression, the simplest classifier there is, which reaches nought point five six nine.

105
00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.320
Our CNN reaches nought point five eight seven.

106
00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:26.760
Is that a win?

107
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:33.960
Retraining the CNN from different random starting weights gives anywhere between nought point five six nine and nought point five eight seven.

108
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:41.120
So the margin over logistic regression is entirely inside the noise of a hundred-and-nine-protein test set.

109
00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.320
Claiming it would be wrong.

110
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:51.680
The robust finding is the gap to the flat network: same data, same split, a tenth of the weights, better on every metric.

111
00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:53.840
That is the claim that survives.

112
00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:59.400
Being able to tell those two kinds of claim apart is most of what makes someone good at this.

113
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.400
Now the part I promised: we open the network up.

114
00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:07.120
We can do this because of what a first-layer filter is.

115
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:15.240
We established it two slides into this block: each one is a twenty by seven matrix, one weight per amino acid per position in its window.

116
00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:19.160
That is small enough to simply print and look at.

117
00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:26.840
You cannot do this with a layer deep inside a large network, but the first layer sits directly on the data, so its weights are interpretable.

118
00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:34.400
So: take the filter that responds most strongly and most selectively in secreted proteins, the proteins the cell exports, and draw it.

119
00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.120
That is the left-hand panel on this slide.

120
00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:43.120
The amino acids are ordered with the most hydrophobic, the most water-avoiding, at the top.

121
00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.720
And look where the large positive weights are.

122
00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.440
They are at the top.

123
00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:53.480
This filter has learned to fire on a run of water-repelling amino acids.

124
00:11:53.480 --> 00:12:02.000
Then the right-hand panel asks a different question: where along the sequence does this filter fire most strongly?

125
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.040
The two curves are secreted proteins, and all the other classes.

126
00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:10.800
For secreted proteins, there is a clear peak very early in the sequence.

127
00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:13.280
For the others, there is not.

128
00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.360
Hold that thought for the next slide.

129
00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:21.880
Let me now put numbers on what we just saw, because this is the slide where the biology appears.

130
00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:26.840
Take the five filters that are most selective for secreted proteins.

131
00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:36.000
Correlate each one's weights with the Kyte-Doolittle hydrophobicity scale, which is simply a table giving each amino acid a number for how strongly it avoids water.

132
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:41.120
The correlations are between nought point seven zero and nought point eight three.

133
00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:50.360
To see that those five are special, compare: across all thirty-two filters in that layer, the mean correlation with hydrophobicity is nought point one two.

134
00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.400
So most filters are doing something else entirely.

135
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:58.200
Five of them have independently become hydrophobicity detectors.

136
00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.200
And where do those five fire?

137
00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:04.760
At a median residue position of eleven to twelve in secreted proteins.

138
00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:09.200
In the other four classes, between sixty-two and seventy-six.

139
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:10.400
Position eleven.

140
00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:12.480
Right at the start of the protein.

141
00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:17.000
Now, what sits at the start of a secreted protein?

142
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:18.360
A signal peptide.

143
00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:35.040
It is a short stretch at the N-terminus, the end of the chain that is made first, with a hydrophobic core in the middle of it, and it works as a postal address: it tells the cell's machinery that this protein is to be pushed out through a membrane rather than left in the cytoplasm.

144
00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:39.240
Nobody told this network that signal peptides exist.

145
00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.520
There is no feature called signal peptide anywhere in its input.

146
00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:55.120
It was given raw sequence and asked which compartment, and gradient descent, trying to reduce its error, built a detector for a hydrophobic stretch and learned to care about it specifically near the N-terminus.

147
00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:00.800
This is the same thing that happened to the DNA filters that turned out to match known transcription-factor motifs.

148
00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:07.760
When the architecture matches the structure of the problem, the things the network learns are often the things we already named.

149
00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:13.000
Here is Block one's receptive field, arriving somewhere meaningful.

150
00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:15.360
Let us compute it for our own network.

151
00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:20.800
Layer one has a seven-residue window, so a layer-one unit sees seven residues.

152
00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:30.040
Then there is two-times max pooling, which halves the length, so each position going into layer two now stands for two positions of the original sequence.

153
00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:33.400
Then layer two has another seven-wide kernel.

154
00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:40.800
Work it through: seven in layer one, then pooling doubles the effective step, then seven more at that doubled scale.

155
00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:47.000
Each unit in the second layer depends on about twenty residues of the original protein.

156
00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.480
Twenty residues.

157
00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:52.280
And a signal peptide is roughly twenty residues long.

158
00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.200
That is not a coincidence that the network arranged.

159
00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:00.960
We chose the architecture, and that choice determined what length scale the second layer can see.

160
00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:11.120
If we had used a three-residue window and no pooling, layer two would see five residues, and no unit anywhere in the network could ever see a whole signal peptide at once.

161
00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:15.840
It would be able to see pieces of one, and nothing more.

162
00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.720
This is the practical consequence of the receptive field, and it is why I asked you to hold on to it.

163
00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:30.680
When you design one of these networks, you should be able to say what length of biological object your layers can see, and check that it matches the thing you are looking for.

164
00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:33.480
The second discussion problem asks exactly this.

165
00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:37.760
And now the bill arrives for the choice we made four slides ago.

166
00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:41.640
Convolution assumes that a pattern can be detected the same way everywhere.

167
00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:45.640
That is true, and it is what has been working for us all session.

168
00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:48.320
But look carefully at what it does not assume.

169
00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:52.080
It does not assume that the pattern means the same thing everywhere.

170
00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:56.200
Those are different claims, and in proteins the second one is false.

171
00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.040
Here is the biology.

172
00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:04.360
A hydrophobic stretch at the N-terminus is a signal peptide: this protein is for export.

173
00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:14.480
The very same hydrophobic stretch in the middle of a protein is probably a transmembrane helix: a piece that sits inside the greasy interior of a membrane, which is an entirely different fate.

174
00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:20.600
And some targeting signals sit specifically at the C-terminus, the far end.

175
00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.120
Same local pattern.

176
00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:25.920
Three different meanings, decided by where it is.

177
00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.240
Now recall what we built.

178
00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:37.040
Our network ends with global average pooling, which averages each filter's feature map over every position and leaves one number per filter.

179
00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:42.800
So our network knows that a hydrophobic stretch occurred somewhere in this protein.

180
00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.360
That is all it knows.

181
00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:49.000
It has thrown away precisely the information that distinguishes the three cases.

182
00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:58.240
So the question for you, which is the open question this part of the session leaves you with: if position matters, what has the network lost, and how would you give it back?

183
00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:01.120
There are several reasonable answers.

184
00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:07.640
You could add more layers, so that a later unit sees enough of the sequence to tell the start from the middle.

185
00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:15.280
You could add positional features explicitly, a channel that simply says how far along we are.

186
00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:21.560
You could use a pooling operation that keeps some position information rather than averaging it all away.

187
00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.360
All of those have been tried.

188
00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:33.200
And this is how the field actually answered it, which is a good note to end the block on, because it is not a trick: it is the architecture of Session eleven, arriving early.

189
00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:42.840
DeepLoc, from twenty seventeen, solves the same problem we have been working on, protein subcellular localisation, and it is built in two halves.

190
00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:50.920
The first half is convolutional, exactly like ours: find the local patterns, the motifs, the hydrophobic stretches.

191
00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:55.160
Convolution is good at that, and it is cheap, and it needs little data.

192
00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:58.360
There is no reason to do that part any other way.

193
00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:02.680
The second half is a bidirectional LSTM with attention.

194
00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:12.600
We have not met those yet, so for now take this description: it is a model that reads the sequence in order, from both ends, and can decide which positions to pay attention to.

195
00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.680
The important word is order.

196
00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:17.200
It knows where it is in the sequence.

197
00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:25.200
So the convolutions find what is there, and the order-aware half on top restores the position that pooling threw away.

198
00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:30.200
The two halves do the two jobs that one architecture could not do alone.

199
00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:37.200
Session eleven is where we build that second half: recurrent networks, LSTMs, attention and the Transformer.

200
00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:48.880
And SignalP, which is the real tool for finding signal peptides, runs through that whole history, from a feed-forward network in nineteen ninety-five to a protein language model in twenty twenty-two.

201
00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:53.640
So we will come back to this exact piece of biology, with better machinery.

